(6394309, 946122, 113142, '<td valign="top">This image is strangely beautiful! That''s a good thing, by the way. there is an essence of falshood to it, something is not as it seems, a contrivance, trick, illusion. when reality can be captured to represent imagination, a photograph truly exists.</td>', 'This image is strangely beautiful! That''s a good thing, by the way. there is an essence of falshood to it, something is not as it seems, a contrivance, trick, illusion. when reality can be captured to represent imagination, a photograph truly exists.', '2011-04-24 23:38:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(6394314, 945469, 113142, '<td valign="top">Tubes, one version of the poor person''s macro. I personally use reversed lenses. best bet, at least from my exp. (notwithstanding my abyssmal rating in regard to the DPC) is NOT to use a tripod out in the field unless you can get your subject to move or move your camera as if on rails toward the subject. off-camera flash would be the key in that case.</td>', 'Tubes, one version of the poor person''s macro. I personally use reversed lenses. best bet, at least from my exp. (notwithstanding my abyssmal rating in regard to the DPC) is NOT to use a tripod out in the field unless you can get your subject to move or move your camera as if on rails toward the subject. off-camera flash would be the key in that case.', '2011-04-24 23:41:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(6394319, 945953, 113142, '<td valign="top">disgusting. love it. well beyond the usual commercial-viable material that seems to win around here. I like to remind myself, the comments mean more than the place. It is not possible to define art, but hey some people try to. not you, thank god! No "trying" here!</td>', 'disgusting. love it. well beyond the usual commercial-viable material that seems to win around here. I like to remind myself, the comments mean more than the place. It is not possible to define art, but hey some people try to. not you, thank god! No "trying" here!', '2011-04-24 23:45:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(6394985, 946111, 100393, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club:<br/> <br/>Well, you certainly made an impact with this one and actually I think the overall scene you shot was a good choice for this challenge. I can even buy the centred composition with the trees eclipsing the setting sunlight.<br/> <br/>However, your processing is a different matter entirely - sure it''s obvious that this must''ve been the look you were going for but that doesn''t explain the choice - the end result is ugly and more than slightly ruinous of your nice scene. I think people have little tolerance for seeing good shots ruined.<br/> <br/>Your other comments are right, halos and fringing are pretty much pathognomonic of badly applied HDR. Having made that choice, you''re living with the results.<br/> <br/>Of course, if you like the look then carry on but if you want higher scores you''ll need to accept that your images have an audience other than yourself and unlike you they do get to vote on your images.<br/> <br/>Now, there is a whole subculture on DPC of supporting alternative and disruptive images and playing to that crowd can be fun but even there, with this shot - you''ll struggle to win admirers.<br/> <br/>However, as I said before - if you''re just shooting for yourself then carry on, that''s admirable in itself and after all, it is your image.<br/> <br/>Paul</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club: Well, you certainly made an impact with this one and actually I think the overall scene you shot was a good choice for this challenge. I can even buy the centred composition with the trees eclipsing the setting sunlight. However, your processing is a different matter entirely - sure it''s obvious that this must''ve been the look you were going for but that doesn''t explain the choice - the end result is ugly and more than slightly ruinous of your nice scene. I think people have little tolerance for seeing good shots ruined. Your other comments are right, halos and fringing are pretty much pathognomonic of badly applied HDR. Having made that choice, you''re living with the results. Of course, if you like the look then carry on but if you want higher scores you''ll need to accept that your images have an audience other than yourself and unlike you they do get to vote on your images. Now, there is a whole subculture on DPC of supporting alternative and disruptive images and playing to that crowd can be fun but even there, with this shot - you''ll struggle to win admirers. However, as I said before - if you''re just shooting for yourself then carry on, that''s admirable in itself and after all, it is your image. Paul', '2011-04-25 13:47:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(6395028, 945141, 100393, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club:<br/> <br/>Well done for entering your first image in a DPC challenge. I like idea of this image, your notes explain your intention well. The idea is a third of the job with the other two thirds being execution and presentation.<br/> <br/>Execution: Focus-wise, this image struggles - I''m not sure what''s caused this lack of focus but you''ve missed it by quite some margin. Working with an aperture of f/5.6 doesn''t give you a huge depth of field at short object to camera distances so the tolerance for missing the focus is small.<br/> <br/>I''m not sure why you are set to 1600ISO, although I can''t see any evidence of noise, with a shutter speed of 1/2500, you have plenty of margin there to bring both down.<br/> <br/>Compositionally, the basics are there but as you commenters have said either include all of the flower or go closer; here it just looks like a petal has been carelessly clipped. I suspect that you might have been inside your lens''s minimum focussing distance so pulling back a bit would help with composition and focus.<br/> <br/>Exposure wise it''s tricky high contrast lighting and although it isn''t optimal on the screen, I don''t think you''re so far away that you couldn''t pull it back in software.<br/> <br/>Presentation: (Post processing): Not sure what tools you have access to here but doing some highlight recovery and adding some tonal contrast to the petals would help the overall quality of the image. Having said that, with an image this unsharp this alone wouldn''t make much difference - you need to raw material first.<br/> <br/>Good luck with your next entry.<br/> <br/>Paul</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club: Well done for entering your first image in a DPC challenge. I like idea of this image, your notes explain your intention well. The idea is a third of the job with the other two thirds being execution and presentation. Execution: Focus-wise, this image struggles - I''m not sure what''s caused this lack of focus but you''ve missed it by quite some margin. Working with an aperture of f/5.6 doesn''t give you a huge depth of field at short object to camera distances so the tolerance for missing the focus is small. I''m not sure why you are set to 1600ISO, although I can''t see any evidence of noise, with a shutter speed of 1/2500, you have plenty of margin there to bring both down. Compositionally, the basics are there but as you commenters have said either include all of the flower or go closer; here it just looks like a petal has been carelessly clipped. I suspect that you might have been inside your lens''s minimum focussing distance so pulling back a bit would help with composition and focus. Exposure wise it''s tricky high contrast lighting and although it isn''t optimal on the screen, I don''t think you''re so far away that you couldn''t pull it back in software. Presentation: (Post processing): Not sure what tools you have access to here but doing some highlight recovery and adding some tonal contrast to the petals would help the overall quality of the image. Having said that, with an image this unsharp this alone wouldn''t make much difference - you need to raw material first. Good luck with your next entry. Paul', '2011-04-25 14:18:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(6395066, 945959, 122892, '<td valign="top">Nice. I never thought to use a "hand" filter before!</td>', 'Nice. I never thought to use a "hand" filter before!', '2011-04-25 16:26:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(6395112, 945837, 19959, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique Club..<br/> <br/>I guess that you might be a little disappointed with your placement in this challenge.<br/> <br/>There is nothing technically wrong with the photo and it shows an element of creativity and<br/>experimentation that is admirable.<br/> <br/>Perhaps this was a poor choice of subject matter in this challenge in that it doesn''t yell ''Warm Colors'' and therefore<br/>voters will have marked it down.. don''t be disheartened.. take a look at the other entries in the challenge<br/>and read the comments... I look forward to seeing future photos from you in future challenges<br/> <br/>Chris<br/> <br/></td>', 'Hi from the Critique Club.. I guess that you might be a little disappointed with your placement in this challenge. There is nothing technically wrong with the photo and it shows an element of creativity andexperimentation that is admirable. Perhaps this was a poor choice of subject matter in this challenge in that it doesn''t yell ''Warm Colors'' and thereforevoters will have marked it down.. don''t be disheartened.. take a look at the other entries in the challengeand read the comments... I look forward to seeing future photos from you in future challenges Chris ', '2011-04-25 18:17:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(6395113, 945971, 19959, '<td valign="top">Hi from the Critique club...<br/> <br/>I actually quite like this abstract photo and I''m not usually a fan.. I think it works well for the challenge<br/>and shows a great element of creativity and experimentation. <br/> <br/>You got some great comments from voters who appreciated what you tried to achieve... it is not the type of<br/>photo that traditionally does well in challenges though so don''t be disappointed by the placing.</td>', 'Hi from the Critique club... I actually quite like this abstract photo and I''m not usually a fan.. I think it works well for the challengeand shows a great element of creativity and experimentation.  You got some great comments from voters who appreciated what you tried to achieve... it is not the type ofphoto that traditionally does well in challenges though so don''t be disappointed by the placing.', '2011-04-25 18:20:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(6395117, 945561, 19959, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>I am not sure why this image scored as low as it did as it is well photographed and composed.<br/>The lighting circumstances appear challenging and you have done well to make an image here.<br/> <br/>Perhaps the girls face is a little too deep in shadow and eye contact would have improved this picture.<br/>The one comment received seems to have hinted at this as the reason that this image was low scored.<br/> <br/>Personally I like it and find it to hit the challenge. The girl looking out of frame adds to the story telling<br/>aspect but I think that it would benefit from space to the left for her to ''look into'' in that case and also<br/>slightly more detail in the face area.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club I am not sure why this image scored as low as it did as it is well photographed and composed.The lighting circumstances appear challenging and you have done well to make an image here. Perhaps the girls face is a little too deep in shadow and eye contact would have improved this picture.The one comment received seems to have hinted at this as the reason that this image was low scored. Personally I like it and find it to hit the challenge. The girl looking out of frame adds to the story tellingaspect but I think that it would benefit from space to the left for her to ''look into'' in that case and alsoslightly more detail in the face area. ', '2011-04-25 18:25:49.000000', NULL, 0),
